<p class="lead">Yes — and we lay a good deal of it between September and November. Autumn gets a bad name for resin because the two things it needs are warmth and a dry window, and both get less reliable as the year goes on. Neither is a reason to wait until spring. It just means the job has to be planned around the weather rather than the diary.</p>

<h2>What resin actually needs</h2>
<p>Resin bound is a chemical cure, not a drying process. The resin and the hardener react, and that reaction runs faster when it's warm and slower when it's cold. Below about 5°C it slows to the point where the surface stays soft far longer than it should — and a surface that's still soft is vulnerable to anything that lands on it.</p>
<p>Rain is the other half of it. Water on uncured resin can cause the finish to bloom — a cloudy, patchy look — or lift in places. Once it's cured, rain is irrelevant; resin bound is permeable and drains straight through. It's that window between laying and curing that matters.</p>

<h2>How we deal with cooler weather: a catalyst</h2>
<p>For autumn and winter work we add a <strong>catalyst</strong> to the mix. It accelerates the reaction so the surface goes hard faster at lower temperatures — pulling the cure back towards what you'd get on a mild summer day rather than leaving it to crawl.</p>
<p>That shortens the window where the drive is exposed, which is exactly the risk in autumn. It also means a shorter wait before you can walk on it and before you can get the car back on.</p>
<p>The trade-off is working time. A catalysed mix goes off quicker in the barrow and on the ground, so it has to be laid and trowelled faster. That's a crew issue rather than a quality one — it's why we don't subcontract this part of the work.</p>

<div class="art-callout">
  <h3>What we look for in an autumn window</h3>
  <p><strong>A clear, dry day</strong> with no rain forecast through the cure. <strong>Ground that's dry</strong> — the base underneath matters as much as the sky. <strong>Temperature holding above 5°C</strong>, including overnight. Get those three and an October install is as good as a June one.</p>
</div>

<h2>We work to the forecast, not the date</h2>
<p>This is the part that matters most, and it's where corners get cut in the trade. If the forecast turns on the morning we're due to lay, we don't lay. We'll have the base prepared and the edges in, and we'll come back when the window is right.</p>
<p>It can be frustrating to have a date move by a few days. It's a great deal less frustrating than living with a bloomed finish on a drive that's meant to last twenty years, and no amount of catalyst makes it sensible to pour resin into rain.</p>

<h2>Why autumn is worth considering</h2>
<p>Two practical reasons. Summer books up first, so autumn dates are easier to get — if you want the drive done before Christmas, autumn is realistically your window. And the groundwork that comes first, the dig-out, the sub-base, the edgings, carries on in most conditions. It's only the resin layer itself that's weather-dependent.</p>
<p>The one thing we'd say: don't leave it to the last week of November and hope. The earlier in autumn you book, the more good windows there are to choose from.</p>

<h2>What about winter?</h2>
<p>Not off the table, but harder. Short days, cold nights and long wet spells mean fewer usable windows, and sometimes none at all for a fortnight. Block paving and gravel aren't weather-dependent in the same way, so if your heart is set on work over winter, those are the easier options — or we book the resin for early spring and get the groundwork done in the meantime.</p>

<h2>In short</h2>
<p>Autumn resin is normal work, not a compromise: a catalysed mix, a dry base, a clear day and a crew who'll move a date rather than force one. If you're weighing it up for this side of Christmas, get in touch and we'll tell you honestly what the window looks like.</p>
